It implements ADS, Beckhoff's Automation Device Specification, as Beckhoff
Automation GmbH & Co. KG documents it in the Beckhoff Information System,
and Secure ADS as Beckhoff's manual "TwinCAT 3 Secure ADS" describes it,
with what goes inside the TLS connection as described by the README of
https://github.com/kevinherron/beckhoff-secure-ads (MIT License,
Copyright (c) 2026 Kevin Herron). No text or code of those is included.

Finding devices and adding routes over UDP port 48899 follows the UDP code
of Beckhoff's own ADS library, https://github.com/Beckhoff/ADS (MIT
License; AdsLib/AdsLib.cpp, Copyright (c) 2021 - 2022 Beckhoff Automation
GmbH & Co. KG), which was read to write it. None of its code is included.

Deleting routes over UDP port 48899 follows Apache PLC4X's description of
ADS discovery, protocols/ads/src/main/resources/protocols/ads/
ads-discovery.mspec in https://github.com/apache/plc4x (Apache License 2.0,
The Apache Software Foundation), which was read to write it. None of its
text is included.

Listing routes reads the index group SYSTEMSERVICE_ENUMREMOTE (803) of
Beckhoff's Tc2_Utilities PLC library, as TwinCAT shows its constants; the
layout of an entry is as a TwinCAT 3.1 4026 system answers.

Reading a system's host name, its network adapters and the address of a
host name reads the index groups SYSTEMSERVICE_IPHOSTNAME (702) and
SYSTEMSERVICE_IPHELPERAPI (701), with the offsets IPHELPERAPI_ADAPTERSINFO
(1) and IPHELPERAPI_IPADDRBYHOSTNAME (4), of Beckhoff's Tc2_Utilities PLC
library, as TwinCAT shows its constants. That 702 is a read of 256 bytes on
AMS port 10000, answered with a string in Windows-1252 and a zero after it,
follows source/Log4Tc/Mbc.Log4Tc.Receiver/AdsHostnameService.cs in
https://github.com/mbc-engineering/log4TC (Apache License 2.0), a logging
tool for TwinCAT, which was read to write it; none of its code is
included. The list of adapters is the IP_ADAPTER_INFO structure of
Windows' GetAdaptersInfo, as Microsoft documents it; how it is laid out,
and the answers of 701, are as a TwinCAT 3.1 4026 system answers.

Strings of one byte to a character are converted by Codepagex,
https://github.com/tallakt/codepagex (Apache License 2.0, Copyright Tallak
Tveide), a dependency, which compiles the code pages yaads uses into it
from the mapping tables of the Unicode Consortium,
https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/, as Codepagex ships them. None of
Codepagex's code is included in yaads' source.

